# Constructors, `this`, and overloading

## Start life in a valid state

Class name, **no return type**; runs once at `new`:

```java
public BankAccount(String owner, long opening) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.balance = opening;
}
var a = new BankAccount("Ada", 100);
```

Require what's needed → **no half-built object**. With `private final` fields, valid & immutable from birth.

## The default constructor

- No constructor written → Java gives a free no-arg one
- Write **any** constructor → the free one disappears

## Delegate & overload

```java
public BankAccount(String owner) {
    this(owner, 0);   // this(...) — first statement
}
```

- **Overload** — same name, different parameters; compiler picks
- Offer only the constructors that are **genuinely valid** ways to build it
